WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:07.350
All right. So, um, I did have emergency appendix
removal, uh, late last week, very early in

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.910
the morning but late last week and it was
not fun and big thank you to Pat for filling

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:17.609
in for me and for handling the bonus shows
and getting the shows out and even doing a

00:00:17.609 --> 00:00:19.199
few stories on the Friday show.

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:23.091
He did a really great job in rolling with
the punches. The whole team did in fact. And

00:00:23.091 --> 00:00:29.720
um, incredibly the Wednesday show I did suffering
from appendicitis without even knowing it.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.560
And I had woken up but two, three in the morning
with just like high stomach pain, um, every

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.120
couple of hours during the night. And when
I woke up in the morning, it got worse and

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.601
it started moving down into the right, which
is pretty typical of a appendicitis, but it

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:48.429
could have been a number of other things.
I did the show and then I decided to go to

00:00:48.429 --> 00:00:54.199
urgent care. And at urgent care they didn't
really do much. Uh, they just like pushed

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:59.129
on my stomach and said if it was appendicitis,
it would hurt more. You should just go home

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:05.720
and rest. Which I did. And by the time that
a few hours had gone by, things just did not

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.350
feel right.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:13.350
So I went to the emergency room, uh, at Brigham
and women's Faulkner hospital with my girlfriend

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:20.130
got there. They did some tests, um, and it
was a, after doing a cat scan, it was very

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:25.590
clearly appendicitis. And before I knew it,
they had a surgical resident talking to me

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.520
and saying, you should really remove it. We
could try antibiotics, but it's not, you know,

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.200
it's slower. It could happen again. Um, well
you really should have your appendix removed

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.899
then by this time it must have been close
to midnight. Absolute chaos at the emergency

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:45.899
room and Faulkner hospital. It was actually
great. The, the experience of something that

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:50.770
was terrible was made a lot less bad by the
great care that I received. But just to give

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:56.850
you a sense of just how crazy it was, the
number of people that were needing to be restrained

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:02.400
by security. And I mean, here's just an example
of what was going on just right next door

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.119
to where I was in the emergency room.

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:11.280
Oh, [inaudible]

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.460
yeah. And it just went on and on with people
saying they didn't want to be held there against

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:25.650
their will and you know, just absolute and
total chaos. Listen.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.349
[inaudible]

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:35.340
yeah. So anyway, that was what, what was going
on while the surgical resident was trying

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:41.300
to explain the procedure to me. And at one
point, I think she said, you know, the anesthesiologist

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:46.200
is going to come talk you soon. And I think
I vaguely remember saying, that's not the

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.530
anesthesiologist is it? And it was a lighthearted
moment in the middle of what was otherwise

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.810
not, not a fun day. Um, so things, I mean
it was like all night, eventually, you know,

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.840
the anesthesiologist showed up. We talked
about the plan. Uh, I met the surgeon and

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:06.390
here's an interesting story by the way, about
the surgeon. Uh, the surgeon comes in and,

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:13.319
uh, I find out that the surgeons specialty
a dr matinees who did a great job, he, uh,

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:18.280
specializes in, uh, endocrine surgery. And
I'm thinking back, you know, not that long

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.590
ago, I read the checklist manifesto by dr
a tool go on day here in who's a great doctor

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:30.930
here in Boston, uh, who also specializes in,
I believe it's endocrine cancer surgery.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:35.849
So I said to my doctor, do you know a tool
go on day? And he said, yeah, we share an

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:42.519
office actually. And, uh, one of the last
things I remember when I was a sedated already

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:46.909
with very strong value, which is great, great,
great stuff. If you're anxious, I have high

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:53.440
medical anxiety, this stuff works. I remember
being in DOR and everybody going through in

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.799
a way that was reminiscent of what a tool
go one day sort of developed and writes about

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:02.910
in the checklist manifesto going through what
is each person on the O R teams, uh, what

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.690
is the role of each person in the room? Who
is the patient? What are they doing? I remember

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:12.500
actually saying, the last thing I remember
is, uh, it seems like everybody's read the

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.150
checklist manifesto and I remember people
in the room laughing and then waking up in

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:21.550
the recovery room and um, it was like an hour
procedure.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:26.670
It went as well as these things I think can
go every day. I've been getting stronger,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:32.900
still not back to, to full strength, but it
was not a fun experience. I think 7% of the

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:40.030
population ends up having their appendix removed
from what I was told. And, uh, I don't wish

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:45.870
it on anybody, but I'm glad that it was done.
And so far so good. A lot of people tweeting

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.480
me saying, what am I going to end up paying?
I don't know the answer to that. As many of

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:57.470
you know, I pay $420 a month for my health
insurance, which is okay. And uh, many of

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:03.600
you know about my $800 podiatry visit a few
months ago. I've looked up that the, uh, the

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.820
procedure I had not including like the emergency
room component, just the procedure can range

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:16.350
in the U S from 1500 to $180,000. Now I'm
guessing that there are other factors that

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.700
affect that price other than just where you
have it done.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:28.020
The average cost of an appendectomy is $33,000
so far all I've gotten is the cat scan bill,

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:35.320
which was billed at $368. My insurance has
graciously negotiated that down to three Oh

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:42.000
two. But they pay none of it because I have
not yet reached my annual deductible of $2,000.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:49.910
So my approach is I'm sort of preparing for
being told I have to pay my full deductible

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:54.780
that I'm going to owe $2,000 for this. Uh,
it could be more, it could be less. We will

00:05:54.780 --> 00:06:01.820
see, but I'm glad to be back and hopefully
a just up and up from here, but not a fun

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:07.241
couple of days. And uh, thanks to everybody
who wrote in and said get well soon. Well

00:06:07.241 --> 00:06:10.540
wishes and all of that stuff. It really was
great hearing from people and we've got some

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.021
voicemails coming up later, uh, related to
this as well.

